WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.150
Every year, more than a million people

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.272
in the world die in
road traffic accidents.

00:00:13.272 --> 00:00:17.013
And many of those
fatalities are caused by us,

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:20.653
by the mistakes we make as drivers.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.870
But there's a solution.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:27.720
Okay we're ready.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:28.613
Okay, engaging.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.130
And it's to relinquish

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.563
control of the wheel to a computer.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.170
We've dreamed about it for decades,

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.910
and now we finally have the
technology to get us very close.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.823
The rest is up to these two.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.310
They're teaching autonomous
cars how to drive.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.920
My name is Daniela Landey.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.150
My name is Steven Lin.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.200
And we're autonomous vehicle operators.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.050
Daniela and Steven work for

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.223
a start-up called Aurora,

00:01:24.223 --> 00:01:27.730
and they're part of a team of
about two dozen specialists

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:30.485
who drive the company's
fleet of self-driving cars

00:01:30.485 --> 00:01:32.752
on the roads of Pittsburgh, Palo Alto,

00:01:32.752 --> 00:01:34.933
and here in San Francisco.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.560
Aurora's software learns
from their expert example,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.270
so to get this job, they had to

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:49.080
be excellent drivers to start with.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:50.620
And to become even better,

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.973
they then went through
six weeks of training.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.140
Today, I'm here at the Sonoma Raceway

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.783
to experience just a small
part of that training

00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:07.407
and to see if I have what
it takes to do this job

00:02:07.407 --> 00:02:09.190
before I hop in the back

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.830
of a self-driving vehicle myself.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.890
Nobody actually, in my opinion,
knows how to drive a car.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.460
You all drove here today,

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.460
so I'm not, don't, I'm
not bashing you, right.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.010
But do you know how to handle a car

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.090
when a car does something
out of the ordinary?

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.040
That's what today's all about.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.973
All right, let's go have fun.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.343
Among the first drills
was emergency braking.

00:02:37.787 --> 00:02:42.401
All right, Daniela, come on down.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:47.830
Since Steven and Daniela have already

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:49.481
completed this kind of training,

00:02:49.481 --> 00:02:52.441
they're here today to brush
up on what they already know.

00:02:52.441 --> 00:02:53.941
Excellent.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.880
And to show me how it's done.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:02:59.763
Good job!

00:03:01.123 --> 00:03:03.532
All right, next car, full
throttle all the way to me,

00:03:03.532 --> 00:03:04.794
don't brake early.

00:03:04.794 --> 00:03:07.146
Full throttle, go, go, go, go, keep going

00:03:07.146 --> 00:03:09.007
guide the brake.

00:03:09.007 --> 00:03:10.668
Go, go, go, go, go, stop

00:03:23.447 --> 00:03:25.560
And then it was time for a series

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.543
of high speed lane changes.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:31.410
Godspeed.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.150
Imagine you are forced to
suddenly swerve on the highway

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.483
to avoid an obstacle?

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.209
Get that brake timing down.

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:44.209
Release, release, release,
there ya go, awesome.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.321
All right, here go, 50 right lane.

00:03:46.321 --> 00:03:48.524
50, right.

00:03:48.524 --> 00:03:50.424
50, right lane, that's not 50.

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:55.640
Go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:56.740
And turn.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:05.350
And for an especially tricky challenge,

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.942
the instructors had us
recover from a skid.

00:04:07.942 --> 00:04:08.956
Show off.

00:04:14.718 --> 00:04:16.420
Oh, oop, Oh my God! Ah!

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.770
Let's just say it's a good thing

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:24.633
no self driving car will be
learning from me anytime soon.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.140
The next week I went to
go see Daniela and Steven

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.703
at their office in San Francisco.

00:04:35.703 --> 00:04:37.536
Come on in, welcome.

00:04:42.813 --> 00:04:45.260
Founded in 2017, by engineers

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.010
from the early autonomous vehicle projects

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:50.110
at Google, Tesla, and Uber,

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:54.900
Aurora is developing technology
to power self driving cars.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.390
And so far, it's signed
deals with automakers

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.503
including Volkswagen and Hyundai.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.700
Daniela and Steven have invited me here

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.538
to be the very first
reporter to sit in the back

00:05:08.538 --> 00:05:10.833
of one of their self driving cars.

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:20.790
It says ready up here.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:21.623
Ready?

00:05:21.623 --> 00:05:22.456
We are ready.

00:05:22.456 --> 00:05:23.289
Okay, we're engaging.

00:05:24.267 --> 00:05:25.280
Now we are in auto.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.040
Oh, whoa.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:27.873
Yep.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.220
Tracking the car ahead, stopping.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.340
So the car right now
is stopping on its own?

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.280
It's stopping on its own, its thinking,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.667
its making decisions.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:46.710
This is really cool,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:48.224
getting to see what the car sees.

00:05:48.224 --> 00:05:49.206
[Steven And Daniela] Yeah.

00:05:49.206 --> 00:05:51.129
What are these yellow boxes?

00:05:51.129 --> 00:05:53.437
The yellow box right there is,

00:05:53.437 --> 00:05:55.795
that's a bicyclist.
That's a cyclist,

00:05:55.795 --> 00:05:56.628
oh cool.

00:05:56.628 --> 00:05:58.850
So the car recognizes
that this is a bicyclist

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.670
and if you see these kinda blue boxes,

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:03.863
that means the vehicle
recognizes it as a vehicle.

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:04.777
And the red boxes are pedestrians?

00:06:04.777 --> 00:06:07.423
Pedestrians, exact, you're hired.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.913
Aurora does a lot of its testing virtually

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.313
within the safety of a simulation.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.760
But to teach the system how to react

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.120
to all the crazy things
that happen in a city

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.023
as nuts as San Francisco,

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:33.900
you need to take it out in real life.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.642
What's the biggest misconception

00:06:36.642 --> 00:06:38.743
your friends have about your job?

00:06:38.743 --> 00:06:41.084
That we just sit behind
a wheel and do nothing.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.120
Auroroa's operators take turns

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.892
as pilots and copilots of the cars.

00:06:47.892 --> 00:06:50.183
Today, Daniela is the pilot.

00:06:50.183 --> 00:06:52.560
I'm looking at everything,
I'm thinking about everything,

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:53.911
planning for everything.

00:06:53.911 --> 00:06:56.380
If the car decides to
bail out in the middle

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:58.380
of the intersection, what am I gonna do?

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.200
See how her hands are ever so slightly

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.690
touching the wheel?

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.857
Her foot hovering just above the brake?

00:07:05.857 --> 00:07:09.630
She's ready to take over
in a fraction of a second,

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.305
whenever she or the car
senses she needs to.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:14.420
And that's a bailout.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:15.253
Oh.

00:07:15.253 --> 00:07:16.470
And I took over.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.184
The car saw something
that was not accurate

00:07:19.184 --> 00:07:21.173
and it gives me control.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.565
As Daniela's copilot today,

00:07:28.565 --> 00:07:31.445
Steven's job is to keep
an eye on his laptop

00:07:31.445 --> 00:07:32.910
that shows what the car see.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.400
Getting a false positive

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.600
on the left, flickering.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:39.150
He alerts Daniela to the things

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.515
she might not see with the naked eye

00:07:41.515 --> 00:07:44.630
and prepares her for what
the self-driving system

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.320
is about to do next.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.571
It's gonna want a left lane change.

00:07:47.571 --> 00:07:48.404
Okay.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.730
As the copilot, Steven
is also taking notes

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:55.743
on when Daniela needs to intervene.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.493
Later on the engineers
will pour over this data,

00:08:00.493 --> 00:08:03.490
so they can figure out what went wrong.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:06.264
This is all part of
the painstaking process

00:08:06.264 --> 00:08:09.180
of teaching a computer how to drive.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.541
It's brain, it's a little
baby brain that learns--

00:08:11.541 --> 00:08:13.800
That you are nurturing, step by step.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.952
Um, hm, yeah, I call all
of them my baby robots.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:20.430
I swear, because we're teaching them.

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.820
Each operator at Aurora
has responsibilities

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.666
outside the car, too.

00:08:38.666 --> 00:08:41.940
Steven assigns operators
to drive the routes

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.913
that engineers needs them to drive.

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:47.860
And Daniela liaises with the
team that builds the maps

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.363
the vehicles rely on.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.040
Because driverless technology is so new,

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:00.300
operators come from all
kinds of backgrounds.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.580
And the job doesn't
require a special degree,

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.177
or prior industry experience.

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:07.727
Daniela was a surgeon in Mexico

00:09:07.727 --> 00:09:09.500
and worked for the fire service

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.488
after she move to the states.

00:09:11.488 --> 00:09:15.053
And Steven was a military
police sergeant in the army.

00:09:16.771 --> 00:09:21.771
Operators at Aurora earn
about $30-$40 an hour.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:24.460
And as full time employees their benefits

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:26.589
include health care, equity,

00:09:26.589 --> 00:09:30.283
and fitting of the tech
company stereotype, free lunch.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:34.109
You know you guys are
teaching cars how to drive

00:09:34.109 --> 00:09:38.350
and if you're successful that is going

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:41.437
to replace a lot of jobs out there,

00:09:41.437 --> 00:09:43.976
Uber drivers for example.

00:09:43.976 --> 00:09:48.580
That's millions of jobs,
how do you think about that?

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:50.830
We've had interactions
where people tell us

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:53.340
you're taking jobs away from people,

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:54.960
but what I like to say is,

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.420
imagine you're living in a world

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.344
where you don't have to
worry about drunk drivers,

00:09:59.344 --> 00:10:01.290
you don't have to worry about people

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.700
who are texting on their
phones, and, you know,

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:04.730
just being reckless.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:08.445
Yeah, we might lose some cab driver jobs

00:10:08.445 --> 00:10:10.741
But will it save millions of lives?

00:10:10.741 --> 00:10:11.574
Right.

00:10:11.574 --> 00:10:12.407
In the long run.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.070
In the future, our cars aren't going

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:20.010
to need the kind of intensive teaching

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:22.603
that Steven and Daniela are doing now.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:30.380
But when it comes to the most high stakes

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.370
life or death responsibilities

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:34.449
that we'll soon entrust to computers,

00:10:34.449 --> 00:10:36.510
we're gonna want human experts

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:40.510
to be vetting those capabilities
for a very long time.

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:44.970
We're still gonna be testing,
for perhaps new features,

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:48.130
new avenues, new ways to
use self-driving technology

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:51.018
in other type of vehicles,
so I don't believe

00:10:51.018 --> 00:10:53.573
this job will disappear anytime soon.

